{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>For you have not yet come.</b> For you have not yet come during all those fourteen years. <b>Unto the rest,</b> this is Shiloh. <b>And unto the inheritance,</b> this is Jerusalem." "<b>And one should ask</b> why Scripture mentioned Shiloh here — it would have been enough to mention Jerusalem, which is the principal, and say: 'For you have not yet come unto the inheritance.' But the matter is that Israel in the wilderness would offer on a private altar (במה); each and every individual would offer his sacrifice on his own rooftop. Once the Tabernacle was erected, the private altars were forbidden, as it says (Leviticus 17:4): ״ואל פתח אהל מועד לא הביאו״. When Israel entered and came to Gilgal, they set up the wilderness Tabernacle at Gilgal, and then the private altars were permitted at Gilgal, because Scripture made the prohibition of private altars contingent on the wilderness camps, as it is written (ibid.): ״איש איש אשר ישחט מחוץ למחנה״. And when they entered the land and the camps ceased, the private altars were permitted as they had been before the Tabernacle was erected." "<b>And you should know</b> that at Gilgal there was no built house, but they set up there the wilderness Tabernacle. But when they came to Shiloh, where there was a built house, it was called 'the House of YHWH,' as it says (1 Samuel 1:24): ״ותביאהו בית ה׳ שילה״, and it was called a 'dwelling' (משכן), as it says (Psalms 78:60): ״ויטוש משכן שילה״ — then the private altars were forbidden. Since Scripture mentioned here: 'For you have not yet come unto the rest,' Scripture thereby informed us that from the time they would arrive at Shiloh, which is called 'rest,' the private altars would be forbidden — but not at Gilgal. And therefore Scripture needed to mention Shiloh, to grant permit to Gilgal, where the private altars were permitted all the days they stayed there — that is, the seven years of conquest and the seven years of division. When Shiloh was destroyed, they came to Nov and set up there the wilderness Tabernacle, and the private altars were permitted there. When Nov was destroyed, they came to Giv'on, and they stayed at Nov and Giv'on fifty-seven years, and they were permitted to offer on private altars at Nov and Giv'on. And this is why it mentioned 'unto the inheritance,' which is Jerusalem, where the private altars were forbidden — to grant permit to Nov and Giv'on. And therefore Scripture mentioned Shiloh and Jerusalem, to tell you that it is permitted to offer on a private altar between them, between 'the rest' and 'the inheritance' — and that is Nov and Giv'on. And so did the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expound: 'Unto the rest and unto the inheritance' — why did Scripture divide them? To grant permit between this and that. And the reason it called Shiloh 'rest' is from ״והניח לכם מכל אויביכם״, and Jerusalem 'inheritance' from what is written (Psalms 94:14): ״כי לא יטוש ה׳ עמו ונחלתו לא יעזוב״ — for thus it mentions above (Psalms 132:13): ״כי בחר ה׳ בציון אוה למושב לו״, ״כי יעקב בחר לו יה ישראל לסגולתו״ — it means that the Holy One, blessed be He, chose Jerusalem for His indwelling and chose Israel as His treasured possession. This is the explanation of the Rambam, of blessed memory, in his Commentary on the Mishnah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:59.941420148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}